# The golden `error[nested_fallible]` did not have, and CLAUDE.md §9 says
# every verifier check has a test that makes it fire. Written 2026-09-04 at
# panel 110, which measured the gap: the code existed in exactly ONE place,
# `selfhost/parse/type.hero:55`, with one in-module test at `:390`, no golden
# anywhere under `tests/golden/`, and no sentence in the spec — so a refactor
# could have deleted the refusal in silence.
#
# **The spec sentence was proposed at that sitting and VETOED by two seats**,
# so this file is the whole record of the rule until the sitting the veto
# names has ruled. The reason is worth carrying here: the refusal is of the
# WRITTEN form only. `m: {str: i64?}` is accepted and `m[k]` hands back a
# doubly-fallible value that `.must().must()` consumes; the built-in
# `find<A>(xs: [A], f) -> A?` over `[i64?]` does the same; and a generic
# `function wrap<T>(x: T) -> T?` nests without bound. So a value exists that
# the checker tracks and no signature can name, and `spec:157` promises
# `m[k]` returns `V?` without qualification. Writing *"`T??` is a compile
# error"* into the spec would have made the document contradict itself one
# section away — §12's shape, three defects for 29 tokens.
#
# What IS legal is one level of fallibility through a container, and this
# file pins both sides so the line between them is a program.

function two_deep(x: i64) -> i64??       #~ nested_fallible
    return ok(ok(x))

function through_a_parameter(x: i64??) -> i64   #~ nested_fallible
    return x.must().must()

function in_a_binding() -> i64
    v: i64?? @ ok(ok(1))            #~ nested_fallible
    return v.must().must()

# Legal: the nesting goes through a container, which is a different type.
function boxed(x: i64) -> [i64?]?
    xs: [i64?] = [ok(x)]
    return ok(xs)

function mapped(x: i64) -> {str: i64?}?
    m: {str: i64?} @ {}
    m["a"] @ ok(x)
    return ok(m)

function main()
    print(boxed(1).must().len())
    print(mapped(2).must().len())
